"The Supreme Court held that a motion seeking relief from judgment under Rule 60(b)(4) on the basis that the judgment is void must be made within a “reasonable time” as required by Rule 60(c)(1). Justice Alito’s opinion for the Court explained: “Rule 60(c)(1) provides that a ‘motion under Rule 60(b) must be made within a reasonable time.’ Because a 1otion for relief from an allegedly void judgment is a ‘motion under Rule 60(b),’ the reasonable-time limit applies.” In reaching this conclusion, the Court considered the relevance of Insurance Corp. of Ireland v. Compagnie des Bauxites de Guinee, 456 U.S. 694, 102 S.Ct. 2099, 72 L.Ed.2d 492 (1982), which “explained that a defendant seeking to dispute personal jurisdiction is always free to ignore the judicial proceedings, risk a default judgment, and then challenge that 1dgment in a collateral proceeding.” (internal ellipses and quotation marks omitted). Justice Alito did not contest this premise, but he found that it did not support the movant’s argument against applying Rule 60(c)(1)’s reasonable-time requirement. The Jnsurance Corp. decision itself “recognized that the ‘expression of legal rights is often subject to certain rocedural rules,’ and ‘the failure to enter a timely objection” may result in the loss of a legal right.” 209 Justice Alito also rejected the view that no time limit should apply to a Rule 60(b)(4) motion because “a ‘void Judgment is a legal nullity’ ”’ and “the passage of time cannot turn such a nullity into an enforceable judgment.” Rather, he wrote that a party “would need to show that some principle of law, such as the Due Process Clause, gives a party the right to llege voidness at any time.” And Justice Alito suggested that applying Rule 60(c)(1) would not violate the Due Process Clause, because “[g]iving a party a ‘reasonable’ time to seek relief from an allegedly void judgment may well be all that due process demands.” In a separate concurring opinion, however, Justice Sotomayor argued that there was no need to ddress “the potential validity of a constitutional challenge to the ‘reasonable time’ limit under the Due Process Clause,” because that argument was never asserted or addressed in the courts below. 319 eo Group, Inc. v. Menocal, 607 U.S. 438, 146 S.Ct. 774, 224 L.Ed.2d 223 (2026). Plaintiff brought a class action in federal court against the private company that operated the facility where he was being detained pending immigration proceedings. He challenged two of the defendant’s policies, one requiring detainees to work without pay cleaning the facility’s common areas and another offering detainees $1 per day to do other kinds of rork in the facility. The defendant, which operated the facility under a contract with U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement, moved to dismiss the case based on the Supreme Court’s decision in Yearsley v. WA. Ross Construction Co., 309 U.S. 18, 60 8.Ct. 413, 84 L.Ed. 554 (1940), under which a federal contractor cannot be held liable for conduct that the -deral government has lawfully “authorized and directed” the contractor to perform. The district court denied the motion, and the defendant immediately appealed. Agreeing with the Tenth Circuit, the Supreme Court held that the collateral order doctrine established by Cohen v. Beneficial Industrial Loan Corp., 337 U.S. 541, 69 S.Ct. 1221, 93 L.Ed. 1528 (1949), id not give federal appellate courts jurisdiction over an immediate appeal of the district court’s order. Justice Kagan’s opinion for the Court described the collateral order doctrine as follows: A pre-judgment order, to get immediate review, must “(1) conclusively determine the disputed question, (2) resolve an important issue completely separate from the merits of the action, and (3) be effectively unreviewable on appeal from a final judgment.” * * * Failure on any component of that three-part test is fatal. he district court’s ruling on the defendant’s motion failed the third requirement because Years/ey “provides a defense to liability, not an immunity from suit.” This distinction was important because, to satisfy Cohen’s third element, the order “must involve a right that ‘would be irretrievably lost absent an immediate appeal.’ ” Justice Kagan explained: The right to avoid trial fits that description. It is irretrievably lost once trial occurs, even supposing the defendant were to prevail on the merits. And so, in the ordinary case, the denial of an immunity is immediately appealable. * * * But the right to a finding of non-liability stands on a different footing: It can be effectively vindicated after a trial has occurred, through the reversal of an adverse final judgment. And so the denial of a merits defense is generally appealable only once trial-court proceedings have ended. Justice Kagan also observed that the distinction between a defense to liability and an immunity from suit was also relevant for Cohen’s requirement that the order “resolve an important issue completely separate from the merits of the action”; “[a] decision on a defense, addressing the legality of the defendant’s conduct, goes directly to the suit’s merits— rhereas a decision on an immunity, applying regardless of that conduct’s legality, does not.” Justice Alito did not join the majority opinion. He argued in his concurring opinion that “defendants may sometimes appeal the denial of a defense immediately when doing so is necessary to vindicate important constitutional or public-policy interests” and that “this rule holds true even if the defense at issue turns on the legality of the defendant’s conduct.” He oncurred in the judgment, however, “because deferring appellate review of Years/ey rulings until final judgment does not imperil important constitutional or public-policy interests.” Justice Thomas joined the majority opinion in part, but he also authored a separate concurrence arguing that “if an interlocutory order ‘is not on all fours with orders we previously have eld to be appealab